  "finding": "At the Level 1.1 default (window_steps=5), acceptance rate is consistently very high (often 100% within a given 6-seed batch, matching the historical 72/72 observation) because a pending proposal gets up to 5 independent per-step acceptance draws before its window expires -- at typical ~0.5 recipient-side propensity, P(>=1 success in 5 tries) ~= 0.97. This is expected behavior of the documented mechanism, not a missing rejection pathway or a bug: genuine window_expired rejections DO occur (see the window_steps=5 rows above) whenever a recipient rule's member-averaged propensity is on the low side for that seed. Reducing window_steps to 1 removes the retry structure entirely (a single evaluation attempt per proposal), producing a genuinely nondegenerate regime where acceptance, rejection, and dissolution are all common -- selected here purely on that mechanism-activation criterion, never because it produces a more interesting-looking macro pattern."
